The use case is to allow `external system` to directly communicate with elasticsearch with its `external` content (which ES currently cannot read). At the moment for such `external system` we need to start filebeat/logstash which undersand those non `XContent` requests.
Such endpoints accepting custom content-type will effectively work as a translators for those requests

@philkra or @sethpayne can provide more details

@bytebilly Language clients set the `Content-Type` documented in API specifications (the value set in `headers.content_type`) for APIs when a request body is given. Adding handlers for additional Content Types shouldn't impact clients.

> Could language clients perform a per-api check to ensure that and warn developers if it's not the case?

Language clients could check an overridden content-type against a list of known-good content-types for the API, however I'm not sure this is worth it as setting a custom content-type is an uncommon case.
